import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Retro — discovery at corpus scale: sweeping the exposed class","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"graphopt/project/retros/discovery-scale.md","filePath":"vendored/graphopt/project/retros/discovery-scale.md"}`),a={name:`graphopt/project/retros/discovery-scale.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="retro-—-discovery-at-corpus-scale-sweeping-the-exposed-class" tabindex="-1">Retro — discovery at corpus scale: sweeping the exposed class <a class="header-anchor" href="#retro-—-discovery-at-corpus-scale-sweeping-the-exposed-class" aria-label="Permalink to “Retro — discovery at corpus scale: sweeping the exposed class”">​</a></h1><p>Date: 2026-10-14 Context: <code>project/retros/exposed-math.md</code> screened 321 workloads for e-graph reachability — 140 score ≥95% FLOPs-weighted law coverage — and ran the three-arm discovery machinery on eight of them, minting one paying unpublished law (<code>census:mul_unsqueeze</code>, −12.27% on DeltaRuleBlock). Nobody had run discovery across the rest of the exposed class. This retro runs <code>lab/screen/discovery_scale.py</code> — a budgeted sweep driver over <code>exposed_discovery.py</code>&#39;s arms — on the top 60 eligible models and asks the corpus-level question the project thesis lives on: <strong>how much unpublished paying structure actually exists in the exposed class?</strong></p><p><strong>Headline.</strong> Across 60 newly swept high-exposure models (58 full runs + 2 truncated at the 240 s per-model budget, rerun with the enumerate oracle skipped): <strong>zero genuinely-new paying unpublished laws.</strong> The machinery minted 2,101 pipeline proposals, measured 527 new-and-true candidates, fired on 44 models — and the only paying hits are (a) exact re-derivations of two already-banked unshipped machine laws (<code>cand_mul_factor</code>, <code>cand_mul_factor_right</code>), and (b) one rule derivable from <code>ALL_RULES</code>. All four paying rows land on <strong>purpose-built intake probes</strong> — micro-models constructed to contain the structure. On organic architectures: nothing new pays. <code>mul_unsqueeze</code> remains the only unpublished machine-minted law that pays — 1 find in 68 swept exposed models (~1.5%).</p><h2 id="the-sweep-—-lab-screen-discovery-scale-py" tabindex="-1">The sweep — <code>lab/screen/discovery_scale.py</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-sweep-—-lab-screen-discovery-scale-py" aria-label="Permalink to “The sweep — lab/screen/discovery_scale.py”">​</a></h2><p>Selection: screen rows with <code>lhs_cover ≥ 0.95</code>, minus the eight already-run models (<code>DONE</code> — LoRAAdapter, GatedAttentionUnit, DeepEquilibrium, CosineAttention, AdaLNBlock, CapsuleRouting, ChunkedRetention, DeltaRuleBlock), ranked by <code>lhs_cover × strict_sites</code>. 133 eligible; top 60 taken — covering the entire strict-sites ≥ 2 head plus the named-architecture tail.</p><p>Budgets: <code>--per-model 240</code> (a real <code>SIGALRM</code> wall-clock per model — overrun records <code>truncated</code> and the sweep moves on), a 3000 s total sweep budget, a 1.5 GB <code>MemAvailable</code> abort floor, and the enumerator&#39;s <code>--max-candidates 400</code> / <code>--evaluate 200</code> prefix caps unchanged. Results stream to <code>project/retros/discovery-scale.json</code> after every model. Name collisions across registries (<code>SwiGLU</code>/<code>LinearAttention</code>/<code>ConvNeXtBlock</code> exist in two sources) are handled by <code>source:name</code> qualified selection, added to <code>exposed_discovery._cases_by_name</code>; evidence rows now carry <code>term_to_data</code> serializations (<code>lhs_data</code>/<code>rhs_data</code>) so the parent sweep can rebuild candidate terms for the corpus-novelty pass.</p><h2 id="what-the-machinery-produced" tabindex="-1">What the machinery produced <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-the-machinery-produced" aria-label="Permalink to “What the machinery produced”">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>measure</th><th>count</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>models swept</td><td>60 (58 full; 2 with enum oracle skipped after truncation)</td></tr><tr><td>pipeline proposals measured</td><td>2,101</td></tr><tr><td>pipeline <code>new + num_true</code></td><td>527</td></tr><tr><td>models with ≥1 firing proposal</td><td>44</td></tr><tr><td>enum emitted / oracle-evaluated</td><td>15,804 / 9,497</td></tr><tr><td>enum <code>new+true</code></td><td>1,263 — <strong>0 fired</strong> (see below)</td></tr><tr><td>known-law firings (the 11 banked laws)</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>verify_fails / ill-typed fires</td><td>14 / 72</td></tr><tr><td>paying rows (new + true + fires + paid + verified + cert)</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>distinct paying candidates</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>…of which genuinely new</td><td><strong>0</strong></td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="the-paying-set-honestly" tabindex="-1">The paying set, honestly <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-paying-set-honestly" aria-label="Permalink to “The paying set, honestly”">​</a></h3><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>candidate</th><th>minted on</th><th>drop</th><th>shippable</th><th>verdict</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>factor_left</code> <code>add(mul A B, mul A C) = mul(A, add B C)</code></td><td><code>intake:QuadraticFeature</code>, <code>intake:SharedFactorMixture</code></td><td>−33.3%</td><td>yes</td><td><strong><code>= cand_mul_factor</code></strong> — banked law #1 of the 11, re-derived</td></tr><tr><td><code>factor_right</code> <code>add(mul B A, mul C A) = mul(add B C, A)</code></td><td><code>intake:SharedFactorMixtureRight</code></td><td>−33.3%</td><td>yes</td><td><strong><code>= cand_mul_factor_right</code></strong> — banked law #2, re-derived</td></tr><tr><td><code>mixed:add_transpose_l_id</code> <code>add(transpose U, V) = add(U, V)</code></td><td><code>intake:NoopTransposeAdd</code></td><td>+0.0%</td><td>yes</td><td><strong>derivable</strong> from <code>ALL_RULES</code> — not novel</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The known-laws arm independently confirms the overlap: <code>cand_mul_factor</code> fires + pays (52,213 → 34,809, −33.3%) on QuadraticFeature and SharedFactorMixture; <code>cand_mul_factor_right</code> pays the same on SharedFactorMixtureRight. Both arms converged on identical terms — cross-validated by bidirectional <code>_term_match</code> in <code>discovery_scale._known_law_of</code>.</p><p><strong>The caveat that matters:</strong> all four paying rows are <code>PURPOSE_BUILT</code> intake workloads — probes literally constructed to contain <code>add(mul·, mul·)</code> sharing a factor or a noop transpose. The corpus strict-match novelty sweep confirms it: <code>factor_left</code>&#39;s LHS exists on exactly those 2 models, <code>factor_right</code>&#39;s on 1, <code>add_transpose_l_id</code>&#39;s on 2 (NoopTransposeAdd + ViTPatchBlock). These are the synthetic- witness patterns of round 1 — except now <em>measured paying</em> on the probes that were built for them. That is validation of the bank, not discovery of new structure.</p><h3 id="the-conditional-truth-lead-worth-recording" tabindex="-1">The conditional-truth lead worth recording <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-conditional-truth-lead-worth-recording" aria-label="Permalink to “The conditional-truth lead worth recording”">​</a></h3><p><code>mixed:mul_unsqueeze_r_id</code> — <code>mul(V, unsqueeze(U, dim)) = mul(U, V)</code> — fires 4× (all typed, 0 ill-typed) on <code>model:DiagDenseSSM</code> and pays <strong>−14.3%</strong>, blocked (correctly) at the same gate as <code>mul_unsqueeze</code>: <code>num_true=None</code>, guard <code>unsq:d_in_pad</code> — the view machinery cannot isolate when the unsqueeze dim is a pad/broadcast. It is the same two-sided unsqueeze-naturality family as the DeltaRuleBlock find — and unlike <code>mul_unsqueeze</code> (LHS on exactly 1 model), its LHS <code>mul(V, unsq U)</code> strict-matches <strong>12 corpus models / 22 sites</strong> (DeltaRuleBlock 8, DiagDenseSSM 4, plus GCNLayer, CapsuleRouting, AdditiveAttention, ExpertChoiceMoE, FNOBlock, TimeCondConv, and 4 intake terms): a <em>general</em> guarded-law lead, not a one-architecture idiom. <code>mixed:mul_transpose_l_id</code> pays −2% under conditional truth on the transformer-block family (ConformerBlock, Pre/PostNormBlock, nn.MultiheadAttention). None are shipped; all are recorded with evidence.</p><h3 id="the-honest-nulls-reproduced-at-scale" tabindex="-1">The honest nulls, reproduced at scale <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-honest-nulls-reproduced-at-scale" aria-label="Permalink to “The honest nulls, reproduced at scale”">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>Enumerate arm: 1,263 new+true candidates, 0 fires across all 60 models.</strong> The structural blindness round 1 identified is not a small-sample artifact: attr-free patterns (<code>select X</code>) can never match attr-bearing real ops (<code>select(x, dim=1, index=0)</code>). The enumerator keeps minting true laws that cannot fire on exported IR. The fix is still: mint attr metavariables in the grammar&#39;s unary forms.</li><li><strong>False-candidate rejection works at scale.</strong> The numeric oracle killed <code>grammar:FALSE_mul_factor</code> (<code>a·b + a·c = a·(a+c)</code> — a subtle-wrong factor twin that would have shown a −33.3% &quot;drop&quot;), <code>exp(add) = add(exp, exp)</code>, <code>square(add) = add(square, square)</code>, and every <code>*_id</code> transpose/select overreach — 14 verify-fails and 72 ill-typed mints refused, zero false positives in the paying set.</li><li><strong>Truncation cause identified:</strong> the two truncated models (LinearRecurrence, KernelizedAttention) burn the budget inside the enumerate arm&#39;s bounded oracle — <code>grammar._evaluate</code> runs a small e-graph saturation (<code>proposal._sat_cost</code>/<code>_cost_delta</code>) per candidate, ~0.35 s × 200 candidates, plus pathological cases past that. With <code>--evaluate 0</code> both complete in &lt;5 s (both null: LinearRecurrence mints one numeric-false + one conditional select-naturality, KernelizedAttention fires nothing). Pipeline + known-arms coverage is complete on all 60.</li></ul><h2 id="the-corpus-level-answer" tabindex="-1">The corpus-level answer <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-corpus-level-answer" aria-label="Permalink to “The corpus-level answer”">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Swept:</strong> 68 exposed models total (8 round 1 + 60 here) — the strict-match head of the ≥95% class.</li><li><strong>Genuinely unpublished paying laws: 1</strong> — <code>mul_unsqueeze</code> (−12.27%, DeltaRuleBlock). Rate ≈ 1/68 ≈ <strong>1.5%</strong> of the exposed class at these budgets.</li><li><strong>Unpublished-and-paying counting bank re-derivations:</strong> 3 distinct laws on 5 models (~7%) — but every bank hit is on a purpose-built probe. On organic architectures the count stays <strong>1</strong>.</li><li><strong>Typical or lucky?</strong> The sweep says <code>mul_unsqueeze</code> was neither a fluke nor typical — it is the sparse head of a real but thin distribution. Unpublished paying structure exists in the exposed class at roughly one-per-sixty-models density, and it arrives via a novel spelled op-tuple the shipped grammar cannot express (<code>mul(unsqueeze, unsqueeze)</code> from a delta-rule recurrence), not via recombination of structure the corpus already shares. The dominant discovery mode observed at scale is <em>re-derivation</em>: the proposal families re-find banked laws where purpose-built probes supply the pattern, and the honest gates correctly stop conditional truth short of shipping.</li><li><strong>For the thesis:</strong> if unpublished paying rewrites are the yield, the exposed class&#39;s marginal density is ~1.5% per model — the corpus needs either many more <em>novel spelled</em> architectures (DeltaRuleBlock&#39;s class: real recurrent math spelled elementwise, not fused-op natives and not synthetic probes) or the guarded-law machinery to close on the conditional leads (<code>mul_unsqueeze_r_id</code> fires on 12 real models — the largest measured paying structure still stuck behind <code>unsq:d_in_pad</code>).</li></ul><p>Tooling: <code>lab/screen/discovery_scale.py</code> (the budgeted sweep + corpus novelty driver), <code>lab/screen/exposed_discovery.py</code> (+<code>source:name</code> qualified selection, <code>lhs_data</code>/<code>rhs_data</code> serialization in evidence rows). Evidence: <code>project/retros/discovery-scale.json</code> (untracked).</p>`,19)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};